#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Sourced by the agent adapters. Not a verb: config resolves script names
# exactly, so a leading underscore can never be mistaken for one.
#
# Telling an agent when it will be killed.
#
# Two deadlines, and the gap between them is the point. CONVEYOR_DEADLINE is the
# engine's: the moment the process group dies, taking with it anything not
# committed and pushed, and leaving the item marked `timeout` with a run log
# nobody asked for. The agent is told about an earlier one so the last minutes
# can be spent landing what it has instead of being cut mid-verification.
#
# This is information, not pressure. An agent that hurries a review to fit a
# budget produces a green verdict that checked nothing, which is strictly worse
# than the timeout it avoided: the timeout is at least honest, marks the item
# and fetches a person. So the brief below says what the clock is and what to do
# when it runs out, and never says to go faster or to lower the bar. Stopping
# unfinished is a supported outcome — the session and the worktree both carry
# forward, so the next run continues rather than starting again.
#
# The agent's deadline is DERIVED and never configured: one number in
# conveyor.yaml (the stage's `timeout:`) moves both. A budget typed into a
# PROMPT would have gone stale the first time that number changed, silently, and
# there is nothing that could have caught it.
#
# WRAP_UP_GRACE — seconds between the agent's deadline and the engine's,
# default 300. It has to cover a wrap-up and nothing more: committing, pushing,
# and writing the result file.

# deadline_brief — the block to append to an agent's prompt.
#
# Prints nothing when the stage has no timeout, or when the timestamp cannot be
# read. An empty CONVEYOR_DEADLINE means "no deadline" and must never be read as
# one that has already passed: a stage with no limit is a real configuration,
# and telling an agent it is out of time would end every run of it instantly.
deadline_brief() {
	[[ -n "${CONVEYOR_DEADLINE:-}" ]] || return 0

	local grace hard soft now
	grace="${WRAP_UP_GRACE:-300}"
	[[ "$grace" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] || grace=300

	hard=$(date -u -d "$CONVEYOR_DEADLINE" +%s 2>/dev/null) || return 0
	[[ -n "$hard" ]] || return 0
	now=$(date -u +%s)
	soft=$((hard - grace))

	# A grace wider than the whole stage would put the wrap-up in the past. Not
	# a case to be clever about: say the hard deadline and let the agent plan
	# against that.
	((soft > now)) || soft=$hard

	cat <<-BRIEF

		--- your time budget ---
		It is $(date -u -d "@$now" +'%H:%M UTC') now. This run is killed at
		$(date -u -d "@$hard" +'%H:%M UTC') — $(((hard - now) / 60)) minutes from now — and
		the kill takes the whole process tree with it, so anything not committed and
		pushed by then is gone.

		Plan to be wrapping up by $(date -u -d "@$soft" +'%H:%M UTC'). Check \`date -u\`
		between steps rather than estimating: you cannot feel elapsed time, and a
		suite that takes twelve minutes started with six left is six minutes spent
		for no result.

		This is a ceiling, not a target, and not a reason to hurry. Do not narrow the
		review, skip a check, pad the work to fill the time, or call something good
		because the clock is running.

		If the work does not fit, stop deliberately rather than being killed:

		  1. commit and push everything you have verified — pushed work is what the
		     next run starts from,
		  2. write {"blocked": true, "kind": "unfinished", "reason": "<what you
		     finished, what is left, and what you would do next>"} to the file at
		     ${CONVEYOR_RESULT}.

		That stop is not a failure of the run. Your conversation and your worktree
		are both handed to the next one, so it continues from where you left off
		instead of starting over — and the reason you write is what a person reads
		to decide whether to let it.
		--- end time budget ---
	BRIEF
}
